From 5fcfada83e3d26a7b4c674db9ba3365b35ec8d6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yaozhi-local <local@localhost>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 07:07:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] sub: keep color bitmaps off the text layer

The BGRA OSD path blends every ink pixel in software, while the coverage
(Y8) path is one memcpy per bitmap. Forcing the whole subtitle layer to
BGRA because a few emoji on it carry color therefore makes all the text
pay for them: the same 20 s of fullscreen danmaku measured 5.4 CPU
seconds as Y8 against 19.3 s merged as BGRA, with only ~15% of the ink
actually colored.

Split the packer output by bitmap type instead. Text stays in the
coverage format; color bitmaps go to a BGRA layer of their own, which
the VO draws right after. Tracks and VOs that cannot take the second
layer (force-rgba-osd-rendering, BGRA-only formats, the OSD and external
ASS objects) keep the old merged behavior unchanged, and a track with no
color bitmaps never allocates the color atlas at all.

The color layer needs its own mp_osdtype entries because the VO cache is
keyed by render_index, one slot per object: OSDTYPE_SUB_COLOR and
OSDTYPE_SUB2_COLOR mirror the two subtitle slots, share their dec_sub and
visibility, and are filled as a side product of the text layer's
get_bitmaps() call in the same osd_render() pass. Both subtitle slots
need it, not just the primary: the danmaku the user actually watches is
attached as the secondary track, so splitting only the primary would have
left the regression exactly where it hurt.
